--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
3.  What changed against docs/g4-boot.log
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

bootDll loads and runs.  Where the M1 log stopped at
`OSLink ... returning FALSE`, this one reads

    objdll>LinkOK 01a3e578 0030b5b0
    objdll> dll/bootdll.rel prolog start
    ******* Boot ObjectSetup *********
    objman>InitObjMan start
    objman>InitObjMan end
    objdll> dll/bootdll.rel prolog end

and then the game simply runs.  Everything below is new on this target.

ARAM IS NOW EXERCISED.  The G4 M1 log had to admit that `ARInit` was the only
ARAM line and nothing ever transferred.  bootDll's asset load does four real
transfers --

    ARAM Trans 808000 / 80aaa0 / 82da60 / 8a7640

-- interleaved with the game's own `Rest Memory` accounting, ending at
`data num 74000b` and 0x1f08c0 free.  The 16 MB block is written and read on
hardware, through the port's offset-indexed ARAM, with no fault.

THE GX SURFACE OPENS UP.  66 distinct stubs at the M1 seam, 83 now, and the
call counts stop being ones and twos: 97,944 GXPosition1x16, 97,944
GXNormal1x16, 97,268 GXTexCoord1x16 inside 1,320 display lists, 891 TLUT
loads, 893 GXDrawDone.  398,743 stub calls in 900 frames.  Seventeen entry
points appear that the M1 boot never reached -- GXSetChanAmbColor, the four
GXSetTev{Color,Alpha}{In,Op}, GXInitTlutObj, GXLoadTlut, GXInitTexObjCI,
GXPosition3f32, GXTexCoord2f32, GXBeginDisplayList, GXResetWriteGatherPipe,
GXPosition1x16, GXNormal1x16, GXTexCoord1x16, GXEndDisplayList, GXColor1x16 --
and that indexed-vertex, display-list, colour-index-texture shape is the real
work M3's GL 1.3 backend has to do.  §9.3's implementation order should be
re-read against these counts rather than against M1's.

SPEED.  900 retraces in 15.88 s wall against a 15.02 s game clock -- 56.7 fps
where the target is 60 -- for 1.17 s of user CPU, about 7% of one 
processor.  That number means nothing about the finished port (GX draws
nothing yet) but it does say the engine, the coroutines, the DVD reads and the
ARAM traffic together are not the problem: essentially the entire frame budget
on this machine is still unspent and available to the GL backend.

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
4.  One thing to fix in the tooling, and one wording nit
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

M2a links SDL2 dynamically from the Docker mount path
(/work/panther-sdl2/.../libSDL2-2.0.0.dylib), which does not exist on the G4,
so the first push produced only

    dyld: Library not loaded: /work/panther-sdl2/build-tiger-joy/prefix/lib/libSDL2-2.0.0.dylib

make_bundle.sh now copies the dylib into Contents/Frameworks and rewrites the
reference to @executable_path.  That rewrite has to be done by the *cross*
toolchain's install_name_tool inside the build image -- the host's own refuses
these binaries with "malformed load command 0 (cmdsize is zero)".  The two
Snowboard Kids ports link SDL2 statically and avoid all of this; if this port
does the same later, the block in make_bundle.sh becomes a no-op.

`--watchdog` is `alarm(N)`, a hard deadline, and its message still says "the
game is not making progress".  At M1 that was true.  At M2a a --watchdog 10
run reports it after 541 perfectly healthy frames, which reads as a failure
and is not one.  Use --frames to end a run now that the game runs.
